  "prompt": "You generate Playwright code to interact with websites.\n - If you want to click a generic button (e.g. that belongs to an element), use the full `.get_by_role()` path to\n   the element (e.g. .get_by_role(\"group\", name=\"Test Item\").get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Go\")\n   instead of .get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Go\"), as this is ambiguous)\n - Write exceptionally *correct* Python code.\n - You love to take advantage of functions in the knowledge_base whenever possible. You use them via Python function calls.\n   It is required to use the knowledge base function corresponding to an action if it exists.\n - Use relative `goto` when you can.\n\nYour task is Tell me the the number of reviews that our store received by far that mention term \"satisfied\".\n\nYou are currently on a webpage titled '404 Not Found', with the url 'http://dockerized-magento.local/index.php/admin/review_customer/index/'.\n\nConsider the following Accessibility Tree.\nThe accessibility tree is formatted like this:\n[role] [\"name\"] [properties] {{\n    [child1]\n}};\n[role] [\"name\"] [properties]; // no children\n\nHere is an example:\n<example>\n\narticle \"Willy Wonka's Chocolate Factory Opens Its Gates\" {{\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\narticle \"Hogwarts Begins Accepting Applicants for Class of 2029\" {{\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\n... to select the first link, you can do (because name = case-insensitive substring match unless exact=True is supplied):\n\n```python3\nfirst_link = page.get_by_role(\"article\", name=\"Willy Wonka\").get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Share\")\n```\n</example>\n\nHere is another example:\n<example>\narticle {{\n    header \"How can I install CUDA on Ubuntu 22?\";\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\narticle {{\n    header \"How do I install Docker?\";\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\n\n... to select the first link, you can do:\n\n```python3\npage.get_by_role(\"article\").filter(has=page.get_by_role(\"header\", \"How can I install CUDA\")).get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Share\")\n```\n\nHere, the .filter() is necessary to ensure that we select the correct `article`. This is especially important in cases where the parent\nelement doesn't include relevant criteria for identifying a child element.\n</example>\n\n\nTips about this tree:\n - If you see a node as a child of an `iframe`, you must use `page.frame(name=...)`, and *then* access the node (via `.get_by_role` or similar).\n - Some elements will require interaction via `.select_option()`. They will be labeled as such.\n   This is because they are HTML `<select>` elements, and thus cannot be interacted with via clicks.\n\nYou also have this library of Python functions available to you. Think carefully about whether you think these can be used in your code. If you conclude that you can use a function, then simply call it. These functions are all available as global variables.\n<functions>\n\n</functions>\n\nPlease think step by step and determine if further action needs to be taken. \nUse Playwright and complete the following Python function declaration:\n\nasync def act(page):\n    ...\n\nIf further action needs to be taken, provide a code snippet to interact with the page. \nPlease be noted that if you call an API that returns information retrieved from the website, you must return the result as-is, without any processing, filtering, or transformation. Do not attempt to interpret the data, extract values, or apply conditions. \nPlease return the API response results or print it out exactly as received. \n\n\nIf you have no more actions to perform, leave the `python_code` field blank and set the `terminate_with_result` field of your response to a non-empty string. Make sure that your terminate_with_result value is a direct response to the original task query.\n\nYour previous actions are:\n<previous_actions>\n<reasoning>\n1) Reviewed the current dashboard state. The target (customer reviews listing) is not on the dashboard.\n2) Considered UI navigation: clicking \"Catalog\" could be used, but clicking a link that navigates should be the last action and makes chaining fragile. Instead, a relative goto directly to the customer reviews index is more reliable.\n3) Checked available knowledge-base functions: none are available, so none are used.\n4) After navigation, to count reviews mentioning \"satisfied\" I will locate rows in the reviews grid (get_by_role(\"row\")) and filter them by presence of the text \"satisfied\" using page.get_by_text(\"satisfied\"). This is a case-insensitive match in Playwright text locators and the filter will return only rows that contain the term.\n5) I wait for networkidle to let the grid load, then count the filtered rows and print/return the count.\n\nNotes on alternatives considered:\n- Clicking the top menu and submenu items: rejected because clicking a link that navigates must be the last action; also menu behavior can vary and a direct relative URL is more deterministic.\n- Using an XPath search across the whole page: considered but filtering rows by role is more precise and aligns with the accessibility-based approach.\n\nNo knowledge-base helper functions were available or applicable for this task.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Navigate directly to the Customer Reviews admin page using a relative URL.\n    # Rationale: clicking the top-level Catalog link would perform a navigation that should be the last action\n    # if we wanted to chain further UI interactions. Using a relative goto lets us navigate and then inspect\n    # the resulting page in the same script.\n    await page.goto(\"review_customer/index/\")\n    # Wait for network idle to ensure the reviews grid has loaded\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"networkidle\")\n\n    # Count the number of review rows that contain the term \"satisfied\" (case-insensitive).\n    # Use accessibility-role based locator for rows and filter by a text locator for the term.\n    rows_with_term = page.get_by_role(\"row\").filter(has=page.get_by_text(\"satisfied\"))\n    count = await rows_with_term.count()\n\n    # Print the count (the runner can capture stdout). Also return it from the function for convenience.\n    print(count)\n    return count\n\n</code>\n\n<exception>\n'Error': Page.goto: Protocol error (Page.navigate): Cannot navigate to invalid URL\nCall log:\n  - navigating to \"review_customer/index/\", waiting until \"load\"\n\n</exception>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\n1) Goal: count admin customer review rows that mention the term \"satisfied\".\n2) Checked available knowledge-base functions: none are defined, so none can be used.\n3) Navigation: a previous short relative goto failed with an invalid URL. To reliably reach the reviews index, navigate to the admin absolute path /index.php/admin/review_customer/index/ and wait for networkidle so the grid loads.\n4) Locating matching reviews: use accessibility-role based locators. Get all elements with role \"row\" and filter them by a text locator for \"satisfied\" (Playwright's get_by_text is case-insensitive by default). This returns only rows that contain the term.\n5) Count the filtered rows with await .count(), print and return the number.\n\nNo clicks or language changes are performed. No knowledge-base helper functions were available or applicable.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Navigate to the Customer Reviews admin page using an absolute admin path.\n    # Previous attempt with a short relative path failed with an invalid-URL error, so use the full admin path.\n    await page.goto(\"/index.php/admin/review_customer/index/\", wait_until=\"networkidle\")\n    # Ensure the page finished loading resources\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"networkidle\")\n\n    # Locate all rows and filter those that contain the term \"satisfied\" (case-insensitive text locator).\n    rows_with_term = page.get_by_role(\"row\").filter(has=page.get_by_text(\"satisfied\"))\n    count = await rows_with_term.count()\n\n    # Output the count so the test harness can capture it and also return it for convenience.\n    print(count)\n    return count\n\n</code>\n\n<stdout>\n0\n\n</stdout>\n\n<result>\n0\n</result>\n</previous_actions>\n\nHere is the current website state:\n<webpage_accessibility_tree>\ndocument: \"404 Not Found\" {\n  combobox: \"Your Language:\", [use .select_option(option_name) to interact] {\n    option: \"English\", selected;\n    option: \"French\";\n    option: \"German\";\n  };\n\n  link: \"Madison Island\", url: \"/\";\n  link: \"Account\", url: \"/customer/account/\";\n  link: \"Cart\", url: \"/checkout/cart/\";\n  link: \"Women\", url: \"/women.html\";\n  link: \"Men\", url: \"/men.html\";\n  link: \"Accessories\", url: \"/accessories.html\";\n  link: \"Home & Decor\", url: \"/home-decor.html\";\n  link: \"Sale\", url: \"/sale.html\";\n  link: \"VIP\", url: \"/vip.html\";\n  searchbox: \"Search:\";\n  button: \"Search\";\n  img: \"404bannerimage\";\n  link: \"homepage.\", url: \"/\";\n};\n\n</webpage_accessibility_tree>\n\nYour previous actions have already executed. So your act() function should only represent NEW actions you want to take from here.\nIMPORTANT: DO NOT CHANGE LANGUAGE SETTINGS! Keep everything in English, even if you are in exploration mode.\n\nAdditionally if you click a link that you expect will take you away from the current page, make that the last action in act(). Don't\nwrite selectors that follow page navigations because you can't predict what they will be.\nMake sure to continue from the current state of the UI - not necessarily the initial state. For example, if a dropdown is open, you do not need to open it again, and things like that. If it appears that the website is in a loading state, you may call `await asyncio.sleep(5)` to wait for the page to load.\nMake sure you are aware of whether you have already completed the task or not.\n\nThe task is guaranteed to be possible to complete in an exact sense. Additionally, during each step, expl